      "fontSize": 15,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "text": "Q: How do multiple players edit the same node?\nA: Server orders operations; merge policy is field-specific. Scalars may use LWW with revisions, \nchild order uses sequence CRDT, text uses text CRDT.\n\nQ: Why append-only operations?\nA: Auditability, retry safety, collaboration replay, version history and snapshot reconstruction.\n\nQ: How do you reconnect?\nA: Client sends last committed version and pending op IDs. Server returns op tail or snapshot-rebase instruction.\n\nQ: How do you prevent duplicate edits?\nA: opId idempotency key plus client sequence tracking and dedupe storage.\n\nQ: How do you scale a celebrity document?\nA: Dedicated session leader, partition isolation, sampled presence, hierarchical fanout and strict backpressure.\n\nQ: How does restore work?\nA: Materialize the chosen historical snapshot and append a restore/new-head operation. Preserve history.\n\nQ: What consistency is required?\nA: Canonical edit order is strongly consistent per document; presence and thumbnails can be eventual.\n\nQ: How do permissions change live?\nA: Push permissionChanged, reject future unauthorized ops, and close or downgrade sessions.\n\nQ: What is stored in Redis?\nA: Presence, leases and ephemeral routing—not the only durable source of document truth.",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q: How do multiple players edit the same node?\nA: Server orders operations; merge policy is field-specific. Scalars may use LWW with revisions, \nchild order uses sequence CRDT, text uses text CRDT.\n\nQ: Why append-only operations?\nA: Auditability, retry safety, collaboration replay, version history and snapshot reconstruction.\n\nQ: How do you reconnect?\nA: Client sends last committed version and pending op IDs. Server returns op tail or snapshot-rebase instruction.\n\nQ: How do you prevent duplicate edits?\nA: opId idempotency key plus client sequence tracking and dedupe storage.\n\nQ: How do you scale a celebrity document?\nA: Dedicated session leader, partition isolation, sampled presence, hierarchical fanout and strict backpressure.\n\nQ: How does restore work?\nA: Materialize the chosen historical snapshot and append a restore/new-head operation. Preserve history.\n\nQ: What consistency is required?\nA: Canonical edit order is strongly consistent per document; presence and thumbnails can be eventual.\n\nQ: How do permissions change live?\nA: Push permissionChanged, reject future unauthorized ops, and close or downgrade sessions.\n\nQ: What is stored in Redis?\nA: Presence, leases and ephemeral routing—not the only durable source of document truth.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "baseline": 12,
      "index": "an",
      "autoResize": true
    },
